.TH SPARKY-CA 1 "2026-10-29" "sparky-ca 2.0.3" "Sparky CA"
.SH NAME
sparky-ca \- manage Brazilian CA certificates, browser trust and personal certificates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sparky-ca
[\fB\-\-tty\fR] [\fB\-\-gui\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B sparky-ca
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGUMENT\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With a display, \fBsparky-ca\fR opens the graphical application.
On a text console it shows a numbered menu.
A command runs as a script, with or without a display.
.PP
ICP-Brasil is the first catalogue.
Every certificate is listed with its issuer, expiry and state: valid, expired or revoked.
Expired and revoked certificates stay listed and are never installed.
Downloads need verified HTTPS, ITI's published SHA-512 and a chain to a pinned root.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list [installed]
Print the catalogue, or what is installed in the chosen store, as JSON.
.TP
.B groups
Print the certificate groups.
.TP
.BI install " GROUP"
Install a group.
System changes need root, sudo or pkexec.
.TP
.BI remove " GROUP|all"
Remove a group that this program installed.
.TP
.B update
Write a refreshed catalogue to the file given with \fB\-\-output\fR.
.TP
.B auto status|enable|disable|run
Keep the groups you chose current.
\fBrun\fR does one update.
\fBdisable\fR and \fBenable\fR change the switch.
.TP
.B status, profiles
Print the installed certificates, or the browser databases found.
.TP
.BI preview " FILE"
Show the certificates in a .crt, .cer, .pem, .p7b, .pfx or .p12 file.
.TP
.BI import " FILE"
Import a personal PKCS#12 certificate (A1) into the database given with \fB\-\-nss\fR.
.TP
.B tokens [MODULE]
Show the A3 tokens a PKCS#11 module sees.
.TP
.BI register-token " MODULE"
Register a PKCS#11 module in the NSS database given with \fB\-\-nss\fR.
.TP
.B unregister-token
Remove our registration.
.TP
.BI gpgsm-import " GROUP"
Import a group's certificates, with the chain up to their roots, into GnuPG's gpgsm keybox so that Kleopatra and KMail see them.
Nothing is trusted by importing.
.TP
.BI gpgsm-remove " GROUP|all"
Remove what this program imported into gpgsm.
Trust you confirmed yourself stays in gpgsm's trust list.
.TP
.B gpgsm-status
Show what was imported and whether each root is waiting for your confirmation.
.TP
.B gpgsm-confirm
Ask gpgsm about each imported root that is not trusted yet.
gpgsm shows the fingerprint and asks you through its own prompt; nothing answers for you.
These commands exist only where gpgsm is installed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-nss " DIRECTORY"
Work on this NSS database instead of the system store.
Close its browser first.
.TP
.BI \-\-password-file " FILE"
Read a PKCS#12 passphrase from a file only its owner can read.
.TP
.B \-\-allow-e521
Choose E-521 certificates, which Linux cannot verify yet.
.TP
.B \-\-scope system|user
For \fBauto\fR: the machine's update or yours.
.TP
.B \-\-force
For \fBauto run\fR: run although the switch is off.
.SH AUTOMATIC UPDATES
Installing is always your own act.
After you choose groups, a daily timer fetches ITI's bundle, asks each authority's signed CRL about every valid intermediate, adds renewed intermediates of your groups and removes certificates that expired or were revoked.
It never chooses a group, adds a root or installs an E-521 certificate.
Browser profiles without selected groups are ignored.
Selected profiles with browser locks are left untouched and retried on a later run, without a desktop notification.
These deferrals remain visible in \fBauto status --scope user\fR and the user service journal.
Sparky CA never removes browser locks.
.PP
The switch is the file \fI/etc/sparky-ca/auto-update.disabled\fR for the machine, or \fI~/.config/sparky-ca/auto-update.disabled\fR for you.
The result is in \fI/var/lib/sparky-ca/status.json\fR and in the journal of \fBsparky-ca-update.service\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/sparky-ca
Pinned roots, pins and the packaged catalogue.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sparky-ca
The refreshed catalogue, revocation answers and the last update's status.
